-- title:
--   Formally étale Ω̂× G-family of fine moduli points
-- statement:
--   The theorem asserts the existence of a family of $B$-points of the fine moduli scheme $M$, parametrised by the $\mathcal O$-algebra functor $(\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal O\text{-alg}}(O^{\mathrm{nr}},-)\times\hat\Omega)\times G$, which is natural, $G$-equivariant, formally étale along square-zero thickenings, and takes the prescribed values on rigidified fake elliptic curves. The data and hypotheses are as follows.
--
--   *Arithmetic base.* Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r\neq r$, and a nonzero natural number $N$ which is squarefree and divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$ (`hrr`, `hrN`, `hrbarN`, `hN`). A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi$ (`hπ`) for which $\mathcal O$ is $(\pi)$-adically complete (`hcomplete`), with $\#(\mathcal O/(\pi))=r$ (`hres`) and $(r)=(\pi)$ (`hunr`); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero fraction field of $\mathcal O$. A characteristic-zero domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ over $\mathcal O$ together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, such that $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x)\equiv x^{r}\pmod\pi$ (`hFr`); thus $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$ is an algebraic closure of the residue field and $\mathrm{Fr}$ lifts its Frobenius. Finally a homomorphism $\mathrm{vdet}\colon GL_2(K_0)\to\mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) with `hvdet`: $\mathrm{vdet}(g)=n$ exactly when $\det g=u\,\pi^{n}$ for some $u\in\mathcal O^{\times}$.
--
--   *Indefinite quaternion side and moduli schemes.* Rationals $a,b$ with `hB`: $0<a$ or $0<b$, and for each finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ the algebra $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]\otimes\mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra precisely when $v$ lies above $r$ or $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda$ (`hΛ`). A scheme $\mathcal X$ with a structure morphism $f$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and point assignments $\mathrm{pt}$ which, by `h𝒳`, make $(\mathcal X,f)$ a coarse moduli space for fake elliptic curves with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure (isomorphism invariance, compatibility with base change, bijectivity on geometric points, and the universal property). An integer $n\ge 3$ with $r\nmid n$, $\bar r\nmid n$, $\gcd(n,N)=1$, and a scheme $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with assignments $\mathrm{ptF}$ making it, by `hM`, a fine moduli space for such curves equipped with a full level-$n$ structure; a group $G$ acting by $\rho\colon G\to\operatorname{Aut}M$ with labels $\chi\colon G\to\Lambda$ satisfying `hρ` (the action is over the base, $\rho(g)$ realises the twist of level structures by $\chi(g)$, and the labels are unital, multiplicative, surjective and injective modulo $n$). A forgetful morphism $p\colon M\to\mathcal X$ with $p$ followed by $f$ equal to $f_M$ (`hp`), invariant under the $G$-action (`hρp`), and compatible with the point assignments (`hp_pt`).
--
--   *Hecke correspondences and Atkin–Lehner maps.* For each prime $\ell\notin\{r,\bar r\}$ a scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with assignments $\mathrm{ptT}_\ell$ which, by `h𝒴`, form a coarse moduli space for curves with extra $\ell$-level, and two morphisms $d_0(\ell),d_1(\ell)\colon\mathcal Y_\ell\to\mathcal X$ over the base (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) realising on points, respectively, the forgetting of the extra level (`hd₀`) and the quotient by a level-$\ell$ isogeny (`hd₁`). Self-maps $a_r,a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ over the base (`harf`, `harbarf`) realising Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ on points (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   *Definite quaternion side.* Rationals $a_1,b_1$ with `hdef`: $a_1<0$, $b_1<0$ and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]\otimes\mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly at the place above $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda_1$, an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ contained in $\Lambda_1$ (`hΛ₁`, `hR₁`, `hRΛ₁`), an adelic unit $n_1$ in the $r$-th prime Hecke set of $R_1$ (`hn₁`) such that $R_1\cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1}$ is Eichler of level $Nr$ (`hS₁`), an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra embedding $\iota_0$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ into $M_2(K_0)$ (`hι₀`), and a finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ above $r$ (`hv`). A subgroup $\Gamma_t$ of units which, by `hΓt`, is the group of units of $R_1$ integral at all places other than $v$; elements $s(\ell)$ and adelic units $sf(\ell)$ for each prime $\ell\notin\{r,\bar r\}$ with `hs` (four clauses: the local components of $sf(\ell)$ away from $r$ are those of $s(\ell)$, its component at $r$ is trivial, the product of the diagonal image of $\ell$ with $sf(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in the level Hecke set of $\Lambda_1$ and $R_1\cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1}$ at $\ell$ when $\ell\mid N$ and in the prime Hecke set of that order otherwise, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s(\ell))=\ell$); the groups $\Gamma_{t,\ell}=\Gamma_t\cap s(\ell)\Gamma_ts(\ell)^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`); and an element $\bar w$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\Gamma_t$ (`hwbar`). Further, $\mathbb Z\subseteq\Lambda$ (`hΛℤ`) and a coordinate map $\mathrm{coord}\colon\Lambda\to\mathbb Z_{r,2}\times\mathbb Z_{r,2}$ satisfying `hcoord` (additivity, $1\mapsto(1,0)$, the twisted multiplication rule involving the Witt-vector Frobenius, injectivity, $r$-adic density, and compatibility with reduced traces).
--
--   *Data on the special fibre.* A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ of height $4$ over the same ring (`hX₀`), formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of dimension $2$ along the unit section of $A_0$, with `hA₀` identifying the formal group law of $A_0$ with $X_0$ through $\mathrm{coord}$; endomorphism families $e,e'$ indexed by $\Gamma_t$, with $e(\gamma)$ over the base (`he`), and degrees $\deg$. The uniformisation hypotheses on this datum are: `hE1` (each $e(\gamma),e'(\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg\gamma}$ preserving the level-$N$ structure), `hE1mul` (multiplicativity in $\gamma$ up to multiplication by powers of $r$), `hE1sc` (rational integral scalars $\gamma$ act by the corresponding multiplication, up to powers of $r$), `hE1'` (conversely, if $e(\gamma)$ is a multiplication up to powers of $r$ then $\gamma$ is a scalar), `hE2` (for every injective ring map $E_0'$ from the centraliser of the $\mathbb Z_{r,2}$-action and $\varpi$ on $X_0$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is $g\in GL_2(K_0)$ such that each $\gamma$ is induced, on formal coordinates, by an element $\varepsilon$ of that centraliser with $E_0'(\varepsilon)=r^{k_\gamma}\,g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$), `hE3` (each full level-$n$ structure on $A_0$ admits labels $\mathrm{lab}\colon\Gamma_t\to\Lambda$ describing the action of $e(\gamma)$ on it, multiplicative and compatible with scalars modulo $n$), and `hE4` (over an algebraically closed field over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$, every level-preserving $r$-power isogeny pair of a pullback of $A_0$ is induced by some $e(\gamma)$ up to multiplications by powers of $r$). In addition an order $R_2\le R_1$ (`hR₂`, `hR₂o`) such that every element of $R_1$ lies in $R_2$ after multiplication by a power of $r$ (`hR₂r`), with endomorphisms $\hat e\colon R_2\to\operatorname{End}(A_0)$ over the base (`hê`) subject to `hE5a` (additivity on points, commutation with the $\Lambda$-action, preservation of level), `hE5b1`, `hE5b2`, `hE5b3` (unitality, multiplicativity, integers acting through $\Lambda$), `hE5c` ($\hat e(\bar x)$ followed by $\hat e(x)$ is multiplication by $\mathrm{nrd}(x)$), `hE5d` (compatibility with $e(\gamma)$ when $x=r^{k}\gamma$), and `hE5e` (a single $g\in GL_2(K_0)$ giving the matrix comparison simultaneously for all $\gamma\in\Gamma_t$ and all $x\in R_2$).
--
--   *Special formal modules and the Drinfeld comparison.* A ring map $\iota\colon\mathbb Z_{r,2}\to O^{\mathrm{nr}}$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ which is special for the structure map induced by $\iota$ (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`), a moduli package $MD$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hMD`), and an assignment $\eta$ of points of $MD$ to rigidified objects, satisfying `hη` (three clauses: over Noetherian bases two admissible rigidified objects have the same image under $\eta$ if and only if they are isomorphic; $\eta$ is natural in the base; and every point of $MD$ over a Noetherian base comes, locally on a finite cover by principal opens, from an admissible rigidified object). An injective ring map $E_0$ of the centraliser of the action of $\mathbb Z_{r,2}$ and $\varpi$ on $\Phi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ which, by `hE₀`, identifies that centraliser with a lattice comparable to $M_2(\mathcal O)$ up to a fixed power $r^{m}$ in both directions. Finally a comparison $eD$ between the functor of $MD$-points with leg and the product of $\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal O\text{-alg}}(O^{\mathrm{nr}},-)$ with the formal upper half-plane functor $\hat\Omega=\Omega(K_0,\pi)$ of Deligne data, subject to `hnatD` (naturality), `hbijD` (bijectivity on each Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent base), `hfstD` (the first component is the leg $\psi$), `hGLD` (the $GL_2(K_0)$-action on $MD$-points corresponds to the twisted action on $\hat\Omega$-points, in which the leg is twisted by $\mathrm{Fr}^{-\mathrm{vdet}(g)}$ and the Deligne datum is pulled back along $g^{-1}$), `hPiD` ($\Pi$-translates correspond to twisting the leg by $\mathrm{Fr}$ and leaving the Deligne datum unchanged) and `hPiexD` (existence of $\Pi$-translates).
--
--   *Comparison of the two special fibres, and the Atkin–Lehner datum at $\bar r$.* A ring map $\kappa$ from $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$ to $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps (`hκ`), a natural number $n_0$ and a series $\beta_0$ which is an isogeny of height $4n_0$ from $\Phi$ to the base change of $X_0$ along $\kappa$ (`hβ₀`); a full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$. Further a fake elliptic curve $A_{0,w}$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$ with morphisms $a_w,a_w'$ exhibiting it as the Atkin–Lehner quotient of $A_0$ at $\bar r$ (`hALw`), a degree-$r^{k_w}$ isogeny pair $b_w,b_w'$ between $A_{0,w}$ and $A_0$ (`hBSw`) with $b_w$ preserving the level (`hBSwlev`), and `hJOINTw`, which provides, for every injective $E_0'$ as above, a single $g\in GL_2(K_0)$ realising the matrix comparison simultaneously for all $\gamma\in\Gamma_t$ and for $a_w$ followed by $b_w$, the latter corresponding to $\iota_0(\bar w)$.
--
--   *The assignment $\Xi$ and the matching hypotheses.* A map $\Xi$ sending, for each Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and each leg $\psi\colon O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to B$, a rigidified fake elliptic curve over $B$ to a point of $MD$ with leg, subject to: `hΞleg` (over $B$ with no nontrivial idempotents the resulting leg is a $\mathrm{Fr}$-twist of $\psi$), `hΞnat` (compatibility with pullbacks of rigidified curves), `hΞiso` ($\Xi$ is unchanged under isomorphisms of the underlying curves that are compatible with the rigidifications in the stated sense, up to multiplication by powers of $r$) and `hΞdef` (over a $B$ with no nontrivial idempotents and $r$ nilpotent, if the curve is a formal module via $\mathrm{coord}$ then $\Xi$ is given explicitly by $\eta$ applied to an admissible rigidified object obtained by rigid transport with $j\le 1$, the leg being twisted by $\mathrm{Fr}^{-j}$). Finally an element $g_0\in GL_2(K_0)$ together with: `heqΓ` (a translate of a rigidification by $e(\gamma)$ corresponds, under $\Xi$, to the action of $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(\gamma)g_0^{-1}$ for some $c\in\mathbb Z$), `heqW` (the Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$, with compatible rigidifications, corresponds to the action of $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(\bar w)g_0^{-1}$, assuming $\bar r$ invertible in $B$), `hK1` (over connected $B$, every pair $(\psi,P)$ with $P\in\hat\Omega(B)$ is realised by a rigidified curve $(E,\varrho)$ whose $\Xi$-leg is untwisted and whose $eD$-image is carried to $(\psi,P)$ by the twisted action of $g_0^{-1}$), `hK2` (any two such realisations are isomorphic, by an isomorphism of curves compatible with the rigidifications up to powers of $r$), `hK3` (such realisations base change along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps), `hK4` (along a surjection $p\colon B\to B_0$ whose kernel is square zero, a rigidification of the pullback lifts, with the stated compatibility of the associated $\hat\Omega$-points), `hC0e` (existence of a transported full level-$n$ structure for any such rigidified curve that is a formal module via $\mathrm{coord}$) and `hC0u` (uniqueness of the transported level structure).
--
--   *Conclusion.* There exists a family $\Theta_f$ assigning, to each Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent and each triple consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi\colon O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to B$, a Deligne datum $P\in\hat\Omega(B)$ and an element of $G$, a $B$-point of $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, i.e. a morphism $\operatorname{Spec}B\to M$ whose composite with $f_M$ is the structure morphism, such that the following five assertions hold.
--
--   First, naturality: for all Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebras $B,B'$ and every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\varphi\colon B\to B'$, the value of $\Theta_f$ on the image of $x$ under $\varphi$ equals the image under $\varphi$ of $\Theta_f(x)$.
--
--   Second, $G$-equivariance: for all $x\in(\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal O\text{-alg}}(O^{\mathrm{nr}},B)\times\hat\Omega(B))$ and $g,h\in G$, the point $\Theta_f(x,gh)$ followed by the automorphism $\rho(h)$ of $M$ equals $\Theta_f(x,g)$.
--
--   Third, formal étaleness along square-zero thickenings: for Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebras $B,B_0$ and a surjective $\mathcal O$-algebra map $p\colon B\to B_0$ with $st=0$ whenever $p(s)=p(t)=0$, given $x_0$ over $B_0$ and a $B$-point $y$ of $M$ whose image under $p$ is $\Theta_f(x_0)$, there is a unique triple $x$ over $B$ with image $x_0$ under $p$ and $\Theta_f(x)=y$.
--
--   Fourth, the value clause: for $B$ Noetherian and $\pi$-nilpotent, a leg $\psi$, a Deligne datum $P$, a fake elliptic curve $E$ over $B$ with a rigidification $\varrho$ relative to the leg $\mathrm{Fr}^{-\mathrm{vdet}(g_0)}\psi$, and a full level-$n$ structure $P_n$ on $E$: if the $\Xi$-leg of $(E,\varrho)$ equals $\mathrm{Fr}^{-\mathrm{vdet}(g_0)}\psi$, if the twisted action of $g_0^{-1}$ carries the $eD$-image of $\Xi(E,\varrho)$ to $(\psi,P)$, and if $P_n$ is the normalised level transport of $P_0$ along $\varrho$, then the underlying morphism of $\Theta_f((\psi,P),1)$ is the morphism attached by $\mathrm{ptF}$ to the pair $(E,P_n)$ over $B$.
--
--   Fifth, compatibility with the forgetful morphism: under the first two of those hypotheses (no level structure being involved), the underlying morphism of $\Theta_f((\psi,P),1)$ followed by $p$ equals the morphism attached by $\mathrm{pt}$ to $E$ over $B$.
--
--   This is the construction step of the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation in the formal-moduli form: from the rigidified comparison data between fake elliptic curves with good reduction data over the unramified base and special formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules, it produces a single natural, $G$-equivariant and formally étale family of points of the fine moduli scheme of level $n$, parametrised by the formal upper half-plane functor $\hat\Omega$ together with a leg and an element of $G$, and pins down its values on the rigidified pairs whose leg is untwisted. It is the input to the subsequent construction of the Čerednik–Drinfeld fine family from the rigidified moduli functor.
